hvf: ask the system's version on tvOS, watchOS and visionOS too

Apple's HVF library came with macOS 15.4, iOS 18.4, tvOS 18.4, watchOS
11.4 and visionOS 2.4, which is what `hvf/Scaler.h' says of each of its
functions. The driver asks the system's version before every call into
it, through one macro, and that macro named macOS and iOS alone. For a
system it does not name, the `*' at its end stands for "any version":
the compiler makes no check for tvOS or visionOS and calls straight
through. Built for tvOS 17 the library is linked weakly, so on tvOS 17.0
to 18.3 its functions are null, and the first font with an `hvgl' table
calls one of them.

The macro names all five systems now, with the versions the header
gives. On a system older than its library the driver declines such a
font, as it does on an older iOS.

--- a/src/hvf/hvfobjs.h
+++ b/src/hvf/hvfobjs.h
@@ -92,7 +92,13 @@
   /* BEWARE: Only use this as `if ( HVS_IS_AVAILABLE ) ...`   */
   /*         Any deviation from this form (also including     */
   /*         negation) causes `clang` to emit a warning.      */
-#define HVF_IS_AVAILABLE  __builtin_available( macOS 15.4, iOS 18.4, * )
+  /*                                                          */
+  /* Every system `hvf/Scaler.h` names is named here.  For a  */
+  /* system that is not, `*` holds whatever its version, and  */
+  /* no check is made at all.                                 */
+#define HVF_IS_AVAILABLE                                        \
+          __builtin_available( macOS 15.4, iOS 18.4, tvOS 18.4, \
+                               watchOS 11.4, visionOS 2.4, * )
 
 #else
 
